Spec: Unenforced Smepmp normative rules allow M-mode to bypass PMP self-restriction and access memory owned by lower privilege levels.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:127-128  switch_to_next_domain_context
      sbi_hart_protection_configure() return value is silently ignored. If the Smepmp configure callback fails (e.g. SBI_EFAIL from insufficient PMP entries at sbi_hart_pmp.c:151 or :194, or SBI_EINVAL from inconsistent firmware regions at :170), the domain context switch proceeds with partially-torn-down or misconfigured PMP entries. The same call in sbi_init.c:394,473,494 checks the return code. M-mode could execute with stale or missing PMP enforcement, giving unintended access to S/U-mode memory regions.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:116-128  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Domain assignment (lines 116-124) precedes PMP reconfiguration (lines 127-128). Between these two stages, the hart is already assigned to target_dom but PMP still reflects current_dom's entries. Additionally, between the unconfigure (line 127) and configure (line 128) calls, the old PMP entries are torn down (sbi_hart_pmp_unconfigure at sbi_hart_pmp.c:300-312 disables all non-firmware entries), leaving a transient window with no PMP enforcement. If an M-mode interrupt (timer/external) fires during either window, M-mode executes without proper Smepmp protection, potentially accessing S/U-mode memory that should be denied.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:182-185  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      MMWP (Machine Mode Whitelist Policy) is never set anywhere in the entire codebase. The Smepmp specification requires MSECCFG.MMWP to be set to lock mseccfg.MML and mseccfg.MMWP as write-once bits. Without MMWP=1, M-mode software can later clear MML via csr_clear(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_MML), defeating the entire M-mode self-restriction mechanism. The code defines MSECCFG_MMWP in riscv_encoding.h:733-734 but never uses it. MML is set at line 185 via csr_set() but remains mutable. This means the Smepmp normative rules are not architecturally enforced and can be trivially bypassed by any M-mode code.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:139-139  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      RLB (Rule Lock Bypass) is set at line 139 and intentionally left set after configuration (comment at lines 211-214 confirms this is deliberate to enable dynamic shared-memory mappings). However, the Smepmp security model specifies RLB should be clear-once: once cleared, locked PMP entries cannot be modified. With RLB permanently set, the 'L' (lock) bit in PMP entries provides no actual protection — any M-mode code can modify supposedly-locked PMP entries by exploiting the RLB bypass. While this is a documented design trade-off for sbi_hart_smepmp_map_range/unmap_range, it means the architectural guarantee of immutability for locked Smepmp regions is not enforced.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:139-140  switch_to_next_domain_context
      The __sbi_sfence_vma_all() call immediately after csr_swap(CSR_SATP, ...) is a correct and necessary fence. When switching between domains with different satp.MODE values (e.g. Sv39 to Sv48), the TLB may contain cached translations using the old address width. The SFENCE.VMA with rs1=x0, rs2=x0 flushes all address-translation cache entries, ensuring subsequent memory accesses use the new domain's virtual address width and page-table root. This follows RISC-V privileged architecture requirements for TLB consistency after SATP changes.

