  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S:214-232  va: (SATP switch / SFENCE.VMA)
      The SFENCE.VMA before csrw satp correctly ensures TLB consistency when enabling Sv39 paging — this is required by the RISC-V privileged spec Section 4.2.1 (sfence.vma must be executed when changing satp to synchronize address-translation caches). The spec requirement that SFENCE.VMA follows PMP CSR writes applies only when PMP CSRs have been written. Since the kernel (running in S-mode) does not and cannot write M-mode PMP CSRs or mseccfg, there are no preceding PMP CSR writes to synchronize. The SFENCE.VMA instruction here is correct for its purpose (TLB invalidation on address-space switch). PMP state is whatever the firmware established before entering S-mode, and no additional SFENCE.VMA would change PMP behavior. This code is compliant with the Sv39 page table switch requirements.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:333-340  do_trap_supervisor
      The trap handler correctly panics on SCAUSE_LOAD_ACCESS_FAULT, STORE_ACCESS_FAULT, and INST_ACCESS_FAULT. Under the RISC-V privileged spec, these exception codes cover both PMP violations and PMA violations — the hardware does not provide a mechanism to distinguish them via scause alone. From an S-mode kernel perspective, all access faults are fatal by the time they reach this handler (page faults would have been caught earlier at SCAUSE_*_PAGE_FAULT). Whether an access fault is caused by a correct PMP block (Smepmp working as intended) or a firmware misconfiguration doesn't change the kernel's correct response: the access is illegal and must panic. The spec's concern about 'no forensics, no recovery path' is a debuggability/observability limitation of the RISC-V exception model, not a security vulnerability in this trap handler. The code is compliant with the exception handling requirements of the privileged spec.


